In 2025, a US company, Colossal Biosciences, announced three white pups it called dire wolves. Dire wolves were big wolves that lived in the Americas until about 13,000 years ago. The company read ancient dire wolf DNA and made about 20 edits to the genes of grey wolf cells, then grew the pups in dog mothers.

Many scientists objected. A group of wolf experts working with the IUCN said the animals were gene-edited grey wolves, not dire wolves, because they share almost all their DNA with grey wolves. The three live on a large private reserve in the USA. Colossal says it is also working on the woolly [[@mammoths|mammoth]], the [[@dodo|dodo]], the moa and the [[@lost-thylacine|thylacine]], or Tasmanian tiger.

{{story:dna}}

Most seeds fall to the ground and wait before they sprout. Many mangroves do something unusual. Their seeds start growing while still attached to the parent tree. The young plant grows into a long, pointed spear called a propagule.

When it drops, the propagule may stick upright in the mud and start growing right away. Or it may float off on the tide and drift for weeks, even months, until it lodges on a new shore. This is how mangroves spread along coasts and onto islands far away. River mouths and [[@deltas|deltas]], where mud builds up, make good landing places.

{{story:sundarbans-tigers}}

The slowness has a second job. The sloth's main enemies include jaguars and harpy eagles, a bird with talons as long as a grizzly bear's claws. Like many [[@eagles-hunt|hunting birds]], the harpy eagle watches for movement. An animal that moves very little is very hard to spot.

Sloths add a disguise. Their shaggy fur has grooves where green [[algae|algae]] grow, giving them a mossy tint among the leaves. That is [[@camouflage|camouflage]] you don't have to work for. The fur is also home to moths, beetles and [[fungus|fungi]], a tiny zoo travelling on one animal.

{{story:brazil-amazon}}

When water freezes, it forms sharp crystals and grows bigger, which is also why [[@why-ice-floats|ice floats]]. Inside a cell, those crystals would rip through the thin walls like broken glass in a balloon. A cell full of ice would die.

The wood frog lets ice form in the spaces around its cells, inside its belly and under its skin. As that ice grows, it draws water out of the cells, which shrink but stay alive. The sugar inside them makes the leftover water harder to freeze. The frog also makes urea, another substance that protects cells.

{{story:why-ice-floats}}

In 1804, a French weaver named Joseph-Marie Jacquard invented a loom attachment that used a chain of cards with holes punched in them. Each card controlled which threads lifted for one row. Change the cards and you changed the pattern.

Many Indian handlooms still use Jacquard-style card systems for complex designs. The idea also travelled into science. The English inventor [[@charles-babbage|Charles Babbage]] planned to use punched cards to give instructions to his calculating machine. A hole or no hole is like a yes or a no, which is the basic idea behind [[@binary-code|binary code]].

{{story:charles-babbage}}

The big companies didn't use ordinary cotton. It took Muruganantham about two years to discover their real ingredient: fluffy fibres made from pine wood pulp, a form of [[cellulose|cellulose]]. Factory machines that turned this pulp into pads cost crores of rupees, far beyond any village.

So, like many [[@inventors-india|Indian inventors]], he built his own. His machine has four simple steps: it fluffs the pulp, presses it into shape, wraps it in a soft cover, and cleans it with [[ultraviolet|ultraviolet]] light. It is simple enough for people with no factory training to learn quickly.

{{story:inventions-india-modern}}

During the [[@world-war-one|First World War]], Britain printed a poster of Lord Kitchener pointing at the viewer, telling men their country needed them. In 1917, the American artist James Montgomery Flagg drew Uncle Sam pointing the same way, with the words "I want YOU for U.S. Army". Millions of copies were printed.

Both sides made posters showing the enemy as monsters and their own soldiers as heroes. Simple images, bold words and strong feelings made the messages hard to forget.

{{story:world-war-one}}

For decades, films relied on clever handmade work. Small models of ships, buildings and spacecraft were filmed close up so they looked huge. Artists painted scenery on glass, called matte paintings, and filmed through the glass so real actors appeared inside painted cities.

In *King Kong* (1933), the giant ape was a model less than half a metre tall, moved a tiny bit between each frame. This is the same stop-motion idea used in [[@animation-studios|animation]]. Monster suits, rubber masks and puppets filled films for years after.

{{story:colour-mix}}

A top sprinter isn't really running so much as bouncing. Each foot touches the track for less than a tenth of a second and smashes into it with a force of several times the runner's body weight. The faster you go, the less time your foot spends on the ground, so the trick is pushing very hard, very quickly.

Bolt was unusually tall for a sprinter at 1.95 m. He needed about 41 strides for 100 m, while most rivals needed 44 or 45. Longer legs usually make the start slower, which is why his late burst looked so dramatic.

{{story:fastest-animal}}

Harvard's Center on the Developing Child describes the young brain as being built like a house, from the foundations up. Early experiences, such as being talked to, played with and comforted, lay down the foundations.

Babies have far more connections than they need. As they grow, connections that get used often become stronger, while those that are rarely used fade away. Scientists call this pruning. It isn't a loss. It makes the brain faster and more efficient, like clearing a jungle into a few well-used paths.

{{story:learning-languages}}

To hear clearer signals, some devices are placed directly on or in the brain during surgery. In 1998, an American man who could not move used an implant to steer a computer cursor. In 2004, another man paralysed from the neck down used a tiny implant to move a computer cursor just by thinking. Soon he was playing the old video game Pong with it. Since then, people with implants have fed themselves with robot arms and sent text messages.

In 2023, two teams in the USA reported implants that helped people who had lost their voices to speak again. [[sensor|Sensors]] picked up signals for planned mouth movements, and a computer turned them into words at about 60 to 80 words a minute. Ordinary talking runs at about 150. One of the women had not spoken for 18 years after a stroke. Her words came out through a talking face on a screen. In January 2024, the company Neuralink put its first implant into a patient, who later played chess with his mind.

{{story:neural-networks}}

Vaccines once took many years to make. During COVID-19, scientists made mRNA [[@vaccines|vaccines]] in less than a year. An mRNA vaccine carries a short set of instructions that tell your cells to make a harmless piece of a virus. Your [[immune-system|immune system]] learns to recognise it.

Katalin Karikó and Drew Weissman won the 2023 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for the research behind them. Scientists are now testing mRNA vaccines against flu, cancer and other diseases.

{{story:immune-system}}

In May 1789, King Louis XVI called a meeting of the three estates to fix the money crisis. The Third Estate's members demanded more say. When they found themselves locked out of their meeting hall, they gathered in an indoor tennis court and swore not to break up until France had a [[constitution|constitution]].

On 14 July, crowds in [[@paris|Paris]], fearing the king would send troops, stormed the Bastille, an old fortress-prison, looking for gunpowder. It held only seven prisoners. But it was a hated symbol of royal power, and its fall shocked Europe. France celebrates 14 July as its national day.

{{story:metric-system}}

Long galleries around the temple are covered in carvings called bas-reliefs. One shows the churning of the ocean of milk, a story told in Hindu texts. Gods and demons pull on a giant serpent wrapped around a mountain, churning the ocean to make the [[nectar|nectar]] of immortality.

Other walls show scenes from the [[@mahabharata|Mahabharata]] and the [[@ramayana|Ramayana]], the Battle of Kurukshetra and the battle of Lanka, along with parades of the king's army. Hundreds of graceful female figures, called apsaras, are carved on the walls too, each with a different hairstyle or jewellery.

{{story:architecture-temples}}

The old town was divided by a canal. On the seaward side was the "White Town", where French officials and families lived, with wide [[@urban-planning|planned]] streets in a grid. On the inland side was the "Black Town" or Tamil Quarter, where Tamil merchants, weavers and workers lived, with houses built around courtyards and with pillared verandas.

Today, people of all backgrounds live on both sides, and the names have changed. But you can still see the difference in the buildings. A seafront promenade, the Goubert Avenue, runs along the Bay of Bengal, and on many evenings it's closed to traffic so people can walk.

{{story:decolonisation}}

No one knows for certain, and that is why the question is still debated. The builders had no metal tools, no wheels and no horses for pulling. The most likely method is that teams of people dragged stones on wooden sledges, possibly along timber rails or greased tracks. Experiments have shown that a few dozen people can pull a large stone this way.

To raise a stone, workers probably dug a pit with one sloping side, slid the stone in, and hauled it upright with ropes, wooden frames and [[@lever-lift|levers]]. The lintels on top were fixed with joints copied from woodwork, so each stone fitted snugly onto the next.

{{story:lever-lift}}
